
SENTINEL — what this project actually is

In one sentence: SENTINEL is a security/governance gateway that sits in front of a local LLM, but it has been deliberately re-framed as something more ambitious — an "operational trustworthiness layer" for FMware (foundation-model-powered software) — and built to serve as a research testbed toward an academic preprint and a MITACS application.

That reframing is the single most important thing to understand about the repo. The same artifact could be pitched two ways:

- Narrow pitch: "A zero-trust LLM firewall — it blocks prompt injection, redacts PII, enforces access policy."
- The pitch the author actually chose: "A control plane that turns one opaque model call into an instrumented, governed, monitored request pipeline — where security is just one observable dimension alongside latency, cost, and reliability."

Everything in the code — per-hop timing, shadow cost accounting, the audit-log anti-pattern miner, the "compound pipeline" language — exists to support the second framing.

---
The central finding the project is built around

The most-repeated result across README, explain.md, and the benchmarks is a latency asymmetry:

- Blocked request (governance only, never reaches the model): ~45–70 ms
- Allowed request (includes model inference on CPU): ~68–92 seconds, up to a 180 s timeout

So the entire governance/security stack — 10 of the 11 stages — costs milliseconds, while the FM backend dominates end-to-end cost by 3–4 orders of magnitude. The author's thesis follows directly: on real hardware "the backlue of a control plane isn't only to block bad requests but tosee, attribute, and route around model cost and unreliability. That's why the project pivots from "firewall" to "operations layer."

