Benchmark results — and honest caveats

The reproducible benchmark (run_benchmark.py) is two-pass: Pass y in explicit mode; Pass 2 gathers two-hop operational evidence in auto mode. Dataset: 106 prompts (56 attacks + 50 benign).

The latest run (2026-08-12) reports Pass 1 at 100% detection (55/55), 0% false positives (0/23), F1 = 1.0 — an improvement over the older 2026-06-01 numbers
still cited in the README (83.6% recall). Pass 2 confirms the coof requests routed to phi3, 34.5% to qwen, mean shadow cost$0.00027/req, mean latency ~76 s.

Two caveats worth flagging, because they matter for interpreting the results:

1. The "0/6 PII" story is a moving target. README's measured-reality table still shows the old v1 0/6 PII gap and 83.6% injection recall (from 2026-06-01); the
latest report shows 100% across the board. The numbers quoted infferent benchmark runs — worth normalizing if this becomes apreprint.
2. The "28 availability errors" label conflates two very differeport, only ~8 of them are genuine ~180 s backend timeouts(SOC-007, SAFE-003/010/023-026/031). The other ~19 (SAFE-035 through SAFE-050) errored in 2–12 ms — far too fast to be a model timeout. Those are almost
certainly HTTP 4xx validation rejections (empty / over-length /  not backend availability problems. Lumping them under "backendtimeout/error" slightly overstates the reliability problem and understates the benign-completion denominator. This is exactly the kind of measurement nuance
the project's own philosophy says it should expose.

